#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130030} :activity/id #uuid "f74c4548-dfc9-5c4c-bb83-8b7024f32b7f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546 :topic/id #uuid "5269dbdc-fe3c-5ce7-b18c-f9aa0c7f6711" :topic/math-academy-id 2000} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130030 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130375 :tutorial/content "Double integrals have properties that are similar to the properties of definite integrals of single-variable functions:\n\n- **Linearity of Double Integrals.** Suppose that  $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ are continuous functions on a domain $D$ and that $\\alpha, \\beta$ are constants. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[\\alpha f(x,y) + \\beta g(x,y)\\right] \\ \\textrm d A = \\alpha\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y)   \\ \\textrm d A + \\beta\\iint\\limits_{D} g(x,y)   \\ \\textrm d A.\n$$\n\n- **The Double Integral of Unity.** The integral of $f(x,y) = 1$ over a domain $D$ is simply the area of $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}  \\ \\textrm d A = \\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} 1 \\cdot \\textrm d A = \\textrm{Area}\\ \\!(D).\n$$\n\nOther properties are more specific to double integrals. \n\n- **The Multiplicative Property of Double Integrals.** If $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and $g(y)$ is continuous on $[c,d]$ then \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{R}  f(x)g(y) \\ \\textrm d A = \\left(\\int_a^b f(x)  \\ \\textrm d x\\right)\\left( \\int_c^d g(y) \\ \\textrm d y\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $R= [a,b] \\times [c,d].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6158466e-1222-52a1-88ad-028b5c5832c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3860 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "15730ddd-c579-5f89-bff8-85f42db15b97" :step/math-academy-id 22622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130031}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129170 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128989 :question/id #uuid "53185b25-c690-500a-9a82-c18b37668e27" :question/math-academy-id "e-6106"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec44686a-601b-52b3-81df-e57e6708fb93" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445484}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Using the Multiplicative Property"} :step/id #uuid "2526c89c-75e6-5851-84b4-83ae4eaabc04" :step/math-academy-id 22623 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130032}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130376 :tutorial/content "We can also apply the multiplicative property when the integrand contains only one of the variables. \n\nFor example, suppose that $R= [a,b] \\times [c,d].$ Then, we have the general result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(f(x))(1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{c}^{d}1\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =({\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x)(d-c) \\\\  & =(d-c){\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nBy the same reasoning, we also have \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{R}  g(y) \\, \\textrm{d}A = (b-a) \\int_c^d g(y) \\, \\textrm{d}y.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3acdc936-b0cf-5aca-a57a-00bdc558bb88" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3861 :tutorial/title "Applying the Multiplicative Property when the Integrand Contains Only One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "12e3fe87-21fe-5661-a2ed-48880eba8e48" :step/math-academy-id 22624 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128990 :question/id #uuid "a5567cb9-1c16-56a6-b0e8-759edf693a41" :question/math-academy-id "e-6107"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "307bccb6-036f-51bb-99ce-327aad8dfdbe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445488}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral When the Integrand Contains Only One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "e720a516-990b-5dfb-b236-aca8fdfa01d1" :step/math-academy-id 22625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130068}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128991 :question/id #uuid "0cb8fa8a-5be1-5f53-bfde-5acf325f6332" :question/math-academy-id "e-6108"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8059a197-e9fb-5306-8eed-62c0cea88f33" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498492}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Using the Additivity Over a Rectangular Domain"} :step/id #uuid "f422ea91-80bb-5373-9f64-444e0646074d" :step/math-academy-id 22627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130397 :tutorial/content "Remember that the definite integral of a single-variable function can be split up over adjacent subintervals. That is to say,\n\n$$\n\\int_a^c f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx + \\int_b^c f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nIn the same way, the definite integral of a two-variable function can be split up over adjacent sub-domains.\n\nSuppose that a domain $D$ is the union of two adjacent sub-domains $D_1$ and $D_2$ (meaning that $D_1$ and $D_2$ are non-overlapping regions that could share a common border). Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{D_1}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A + \\iint\\limits_{D_2}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThis property can be generalized if $D$ is split into a finite number $n$ of sub-domains (where $n>2$):\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{D_1}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A + \\iint\\limits_{D_2}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A + \\cdots + \\iint\\limits_{D_n}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bc4e2a2c-afd3-57e1-ac95-737c568153b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4258 :tutorial/title "Additivity of Double Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "5249980b-32b5-5965-880e-a7948ef2b5f3" :step/math-academy-id 25886 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129188 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129007 :question/id #uuid "1c227b12-d40b-53a8-b568-b7bd380acbee" :question/math-academy-id "e-7023"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8505beff-75b8-57bf-9312-e0fcf32f44fd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496250}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Additivity of Double Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "bca38320-513b-5017-88bb-f5d1a8c6013e" :step/math-academy-id 25887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130398 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're integrating a single-variable function over a region $R.$ The definite integral of $f(x)$ over $R$ is not affected by whether the region $R$ is open or closed.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x) = 1,$ and define the regions $R = [0,1]$ and $R' = (0,1).$ Integrating $f(x)$ over $R$ and $R'$ gives the same result:\n\n$$\n\\int_R f(x) \\,\\textrm d x = \\int_{R'} f(x) \\,\\textrm d x = \\int_{0}^1 \\,\\textrm d x = 1\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this is true because the rectangles formed by $f(x),$ namely $[0, 1]\\times [0,1]$ and $(0, 1)\\times (0,1),$ have the same area. \n\nWe have a similar property for double integrals too. We can either include or exclude the boundaries of the integration regions without affecting the result. \n\nFor example, the double integral of $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ over both $R=[0,1] \\times [0,1]$ and over $R'=(0,1) \\times (0,1)$ will give the same result:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{\\large R} (x^2+y^2) \\ \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R'} (x^2+y^2) \\ \\text{d}A= \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} (x^2+y^2) \\ \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this is true because the *volumes* of the shapes formed by $f(x,y)$ over the regions $R$ and $R'$ are the same.\n\nMoreover, we'd get the same result if we were to integrate $f(x,y)$ over any of the following regions:\n\n$$\n(0,1] \\times [0,1], \\quad [0,1) \\times [0,1], \\quad (0,1) \\times [0,1], \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nThe same idea applies to double-integrals over non-rectangular boundaries, too." :tutorial/id #uuid "7f51d452-a229-5d76-885e-0fe84f787707" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4311 :tutorial/title "Open and Closed Regions of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "45710b0f-af68-5a20-b9ce-c5ecc560f4e5" :step/math-academy-id 26202 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130034}}] :activity/title "Properties of Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129345}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419655} :activity/id #uuid "abdc4a65-40f3-528e-9141-f38a1d8b8f37" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546 :topic/id #uuid "5269dbdc-fe3c-5ce7-b18c-f9aa0c7f6711" :topic/math-academy-id 2000} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc8e0ee-42d1-5e33-ab3e-d149ce209193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128574} {:answer/id #uuid "216a895e-32d8-5d87-938d-db65b2d1bddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419645} {:answer/id #uuid "4a5a35ab-9584-5250-9b05-bab514871947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19-20e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419646} {:answer/id #uuid "c482d14a-968e-5fba-92dc-75544ab448dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20-19e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419647} {:answer/id #uuid "c6534224-e681-59ea-874f-2267428a83f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16+20e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419648}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419647} :answer-field/id #uuid "5432046c-908f-53bb-aa5e-d0f6af32c211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a9bb5733-5b86-5301-9267-6ae190810785" :question/math-academy-id "q-71994" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,e]\\times [2,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(\\frac{1}{x}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}3{y}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(3-2){\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}3{y}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(e-1){\\int }_{2}^{3}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(e-1)[{y}^{3}]_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =(e-1)({3}^{3}-{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =19(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A=1-19(e-1)=20-19e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8b0d3e0f-96a8-599d-92f9-04874e8f5b73" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60fb41e7-e3b3-596b-9de2-56afe6dd5f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346193} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9ef82d-4456-5d25-ad26-90ed8d7f4c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346295} {:answer/id #uuid "705f8690-2e07-5932-b07c-8089792360e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{T}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346333} {:answer/id #uuid "9666eb71-130a-59fc-8098-2ba78743d703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346666} {:answer/id #uuid "c006f721-3d51-5d16-a6a8-1b8222268571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}(x+{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346193} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef450e5-1471-5ba8-8652-38a2e4337160" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e01cd29-3b44-5c08-bc75-551f046b44dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-80452" :question/problem "![](images/ed/ed0be2aca8d43d733e91f3a8feaaca0649957dcbd516a8218b1e7dea136170d8.png)\n\nConsider the region $R$ shown above. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x, & (x,y)\\in S, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in T, \\\\ {x}^{2}y, & (x,y)\\in U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the domain $R$ can be represented as a union of three sub-domains, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =S\\cup T\\cup U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{S}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}0\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b57250fe-0a51-501d-8a2f-9f787d4986a6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "168581f2-f17c-5d44-a38c-f447560aea9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419644} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d9732d-dbc5-596f-ab65-73c4ce86f71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419649} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b906bf-abf8-5f7b-aa2d-46eb0fb79934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419650} {:answer/id #uuid "da997437-2a62-59ee-9431-4e9d511ef420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419651} {:answer/id #uuid "f67323c5-5cdb-58d8-a3cb-89a513553c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419652}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419644} :answer-field/id #uuid "35e876f8-837f-5088-b5c0-91f0a9d62487" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a49df285-b000-551a-958c-a18cbfff682c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80659" :question/problem "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=-3,\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=5,\n$$\n\nfind the value of \n\n$$\n{∬}_{C\\cup D}-2f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C\\cup D}-2f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =-2\\cdot \\,\\,\\,{∬}_{C\\cup D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-2\\cdot ({∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-3+5) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f337f9e2-418d-5c24-9790-11f1621a546e"}] :activity/title "Properties of Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419643}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496386} :activity/id #uuid "95d8bbec-04bf-5628-a449-ebbd525dd776" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546 :topic/id #uuid "5269dbdc-fe3c-5ce7-b18c-f9aa0c7f6711" :topic/math-academy-id 2000} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424c1725-f6be-557b-8ba1-de6cd2c48a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382464} {:answer/id #uuid "ad38cb99-0e64-5f96-baca-e7d0549cba5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382465} {:answer/id #uuid "1e089d84-f069-5f2c-83fa-ebc8d830878a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382466} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fc8d95-fed9-543b-84f8-e46cb703d09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382467} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1aadd1-ba01-5e68-a02a-c2ed4e7c1f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382465} :answer-field/id #uuid "d86e0dfb-0f1e-5397-8c0c-93b6e0bbf141" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "265058e2-39b1-59f9-b955-fd7951910713" :question/math-academy-id "q-81253" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\, & \\text{if}\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<\\pi , \\\\ x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<\\pi ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(-1,1)\\times (0,\\pi )$ and ${R}_{2}=(1,2)\\times (0,\\pi ).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A= & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =(1-(-1)){\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y+({\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi }+[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]_{1}^{2}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2(0)+(2-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-(-1)+1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5d158e0b-364f-57cd-9861-9ba595b098dc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496370}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21bb7e4f-4f89-5de1-95f7-da1922b3e4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495583} {:answer/id #uuid "4abebdbc-99ae-50c0-a512-0a23de882c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495682} {:answer/id #uuid "714c7021-6f5d-5cb3-9a9a-f76210471846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495759} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e8c850-fb04-565b-82b5-6a4a220870b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495906} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b29483-e8e2-58c0-b501-11ed5e1de262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495970}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495970} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f162e99-d897-57bf-9c7b-c3f9fc5e3dae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "426d96cd-8cff-562b-8539-4a443da3b8d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-80668" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<1, \\\\ 2x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,1<y<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(0,1)\\times (0,1)$ and ${R}_{2}=(0,1)\\times (1,2).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}2x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =(1-0){\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+({\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1}+({x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1})\\cdot ({e}^{y}{|}_{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+1\\cdot ({e}^{2}-{e}^{1}) \\\\  & =1+{e}^{2}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6aea6f9b-1c3a-5b35-93ee-7c684d8d6000" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496361}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496361 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51a89715-7a54-5300-819b-fa080b061464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382450} {:answer/id #uuid "56fb164e-39a3-5e17-815d-ec81eaeb5642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382451} {:answer/id #uuid "a9cb3d91-b5e5-5157-8a43-6dd0b3fbf045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382452} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf0027f-33d6-5dec-9265-dd1640066c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382453} {:answer/id #uuid "5f806e22-cdeb-5e59-8750-426e910009d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382454}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382454} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ffc185-1273-5e29-8ff4-7540cee84426" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c9713fa5-57af-5823-80a4-91a27fbe3a41" :question/math-academy-id "q-81244" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[-3,0]\\times [0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}y\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{R}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(4-0){\\int }_{-3}^{0}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}y\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(0-(-3)){\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third integral is the double integral of unity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\text{d}A=\\text{Area}(R)=3\\cdot 4=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-y+1)\\,\\text{d}A=36-24+12=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7eca13c9-165d-5443-bea1-7c1c3600f648" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496349}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29cb87ec-7b62-54a3-9b91-6761bf4ff971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495602} {:answer/id #uuid "32a207e8-72fc-5597-b6e1-52b672dc0edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495627} {:answer/id #uuid "64babd30-e58f-5686-b182-18182e671ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495734} {:answer/id #uuid "79a8bba4-a404-5ef1-853e-20b00d2df235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495772} {:answer/id #uuid "ec45ef3b-c17c-5ae2-8f29-beba53a099ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495772} :answer-field/id #uuid "25cf7a13-0b14-5e86-98cd-0f8088bad90b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4896f06f-886c-5ec8-9b07-a29fce8c9f73" :question/math-academy-id "q-130021" :question/problem "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=2,\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=7,\n$$\n\nfind the value of \n\n$$\n{∬}_{C\\cup D}\\frac{1}{3}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C\\cup D}5f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{C\\cup D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(7+2) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a4b78fd8-e01d-5b99-936b-9991b9a39b49"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496386 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cd94ac7-3795-5961-a7c1-57dd5b247105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495685} {:answer/id #uuid "683d5a4b-306a-5d43-8f8c-20fb207ba1ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}y{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495740} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9d4d09-68ab-514c-8c81-4e473a25d36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}x\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495863} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b9981d-d744-5f2e-820e-320f4ec86a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495876} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b195e5-7146-58b0-bd21-eaaacb148632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495913}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495876} :answer-field/id #uuid "89e973d3-c94c-5288-b1d4-40bdaaeee25a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3fc1b49a-d775-540a-9853-9d70731cf1c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-80656" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c6e4deccce395f5bdc8aadef8cd2c712804235c340ccae174f2067211a5a49c.png)\n\nConsider the region $R$ shown above. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}, & (x,y)\\in S, \\\\ {x}^{2}+y, & (x,y)\\in T, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the domain $R$ can be represented as a union of three sub-domains, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =S\\cup T\\cup U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{S}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A+{∬}_{U}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{T}({x}^{2}+y)\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eae75d8c-b827-5748-96f3-9fc94f7fde49" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496356}}] :activity/title "Properties of Double Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495503}]}
